WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.558
Polynomial rolling hashing treats a string as a base-B number evaluated modulo a large prime M.

00:00:06.558 --> 00:00:14.083
Precomputing prefix hashes and base powers in linear time lets you fingerprint the entire string in one pass.

00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:21.400
Choose a base larger than the alphabet size and a modulus near ten to the ninth or two to the sixty-first.

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:29.765
Subtracting the scaled left prefix hash from the right prefix hash isolates the exact polynomial of substring L to R.

00:00:29.765 --> 00:00:37.102
Two arbitrary substrings of length one hundred thousand can now be compared for equality in constant time.

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:41.947
Sliding window hashes and Rabin-Karp pattern searches follow directly.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:49.987
By the birthday paradox, a single thirty-two-bit modulus will collide once you compare around forty thousand substrings.

00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:57.635
Pair two independent moduli or randomize the base at startup so prewritten hack tests cannot predict your hash values.

00:00:57.635 --> 00:01:01.848
Double hashing drops the collision probability to virtually zero.

00:01:02.125 --> 00:01:09.420
Because two strings with a common prefix share identical hashes on every shorter prefix, LCP is monotonic in length.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:17.596
Binary searching the match length with O(1) hash checks finds the first differing character between two suffixes in O(log N) time.

00:01:17.596 --> 00:01:24.136
You can also binary search the longest palindromic substring by hashing both the string and its reverse.
